There's a separate question worth settling here too, because it trips up more people than the color chart does. **Leopard print** is not the same pattern as cheetah print, even though the two get used interchangeably online. Leopard print is built from rosette clusters, loose rings of several small dark strokes with a lighter centre, echoing the actual markings on a leopard's coat. Cheetah print is simpler: solid, evenly sized dots scattered without the ring structure. If you've been searching for one and getting the other in your results, that's the reason, and it's worth a look at our <a href='/blog/print-nails/cheetah-print-nails'>cheetah print nails ideas</a> if the solid-dot version turns out to be closer to what you actually pictured. For a fuller side-by-side, the <a href='/blog/print-nails/cheetah-print-vs-leopard-print-nails'>cheetah print vs leopard print nails</a> breakdown covers the distinction in more depth than a single paragraph can.

Once the base is settled, everything downstream, coverage, finish, occasion, gets easier to choose. A caramel base already tells you gold accents will sit well. An ash beige base tells you chrome or silver detailing is the natural next step. The color isn't decoration on top of the design. It's the design's foundation.

